    "narrative": "For September 11, the Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup is anchored by **Josh Naylor** (72.05) and **Trevor Story** (68.66), who both benefit significantly from favorable matchups against soft-tossing starting rotations. **Josh Naylor** is our top play, as he maximizes a rare +19% SP skill multiplier against Springs, while **Trevor Story** continues to provide high-leverage utility at SS despite a moderate tax for facing the elite hold capabilities of Lugo. We are also leaning into **Brice Turang** (68.60) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (67.51) as high-efficiency anchors; **Vinnie Pasquantino** is a particularly strong play today due to a massive +14% platoon advantage, while **Brice Turang** maintains a stellar score by locking down the #2 spot in the order against the vulnerable Abbott. While **James Wood** (67.20) rounds out the strong options due to his elite 0.415 xwOBA and the leadoff advantage, we are closely monitoring the SB potential for **Corbin Carroll** (64.61), who gets a +4% bump thanks to the slow delivery profile of Gore.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Austin Riley** (56.13), who fell just shy of the threshold for our utility spot. While **Austin Riley** boasts a strong BvP profile against Nola, the model preferred the sustained daily efficiency and platoon leverage offered by **Vinnie Pasquantino**. Similarly, we opted to leave **Travis Bazzana** (48.66) and **Isaac Paredes** (44.53) on the pine, as both profiles lacked the requisite ceiling to displace our current starters. We continue to practice discipline with players like **Griffin Conine** (31.59); he remains strictly below our floor of 40.0, serving as a reminder that our primary objective is to maximize daily scoring without burning through our remaining game caps on marginal outcomes. \n\nRisks today are focused on the bottom of the order, specifically **Zach McKinstry** (40.93). Sitting right at the edge of our viability floor, **Zach McKinstry** is an inclusion 